{
  // Loom-managed nested Biome configuration (issue #6031).
  //
  // Everything under `.loom/` is machine-managed Loom payload: shipped scripts
  // and role prompts, installer-generated JSON stamps (`config.json`,
  // `install-metadata.json`), and ephemeral runtime state (`worktrees/`,
  // `sweep-checkpoint/`). None of it is authored -- or formatted -- by the
  // consumer repository, so a repo-wide `biome check .` used to fail on files
  // the consumer never wrote:
  //
  //   * `.loom/scripts/experiments/judge-fanout-workflow.js` is a Claude Code
  //     Workflow script. Workflow scripts run in a VM that permits top-level
  //     `await` and top-level `return`; standard ECMAScript forbids the latter,
  //     so Biome reports a hard `parse` error ("Illegal return statement outside
  //     of a function") that no formatter/linter option can suppress -- not even
  //     `formatter.formatWithErrors`.
  //   * `.loom/config.json` and `.loom/install-metadata.json` are emitted with
  //     2-space indentation by the installer and the daemon. Any consumer whose
  //     Biome config differs (Biome's own default is tabs) gets a format diff on
  //     every single run.
  //
  // Biome 2.x discovers nested configuration files, so a `"root": false` config
  // placed inside Loom's own directory takes `.loom/` out of the consumer's lint
  // surface WITHOUT the installer having to edit a consumer-owned file.
  // `.biomeignore` is not an option -- Biome 2.x dropped it (verified against
  // Biome 2.5.8: a `.biomeignore` listing `.loom/` changes nothing).
  //
  // Scope: this excludes ONLY `.loom/`. Consumer-authored files everywhere else
  // in the repository are linted exactly as before.
  //
  // Known limitation: Biome 1.x has no nested-configuration support and will
  // ignore this file. A consumer still on 1.x needs an explicit `files.ignore`
  // entry for `.loom/` in their own config.
  "root": false,
  "files": {
    // A negation-only `includes` list excludes every path under this directory,
    // including this file itself -- so the config never reports a format diff
    // against whatever indentation the consumer happens to prefer.
    "includes": ["!**"]
  }
}
